 "report": "Particle A42-spiral-e000-d020: knot (k=8)\n\nWhat it is: a particle of mass 3.1 and radius 1.76 with 8 ports on its rim. Each port is a stick-slip contact with a dead zone (mean 0.30) and a 4-dim charge; 10 of its port pairs attract each other strongly (so copies can bind port to port). Inside, 16 couplings (38% inhibitory) pass a push from port to port; it settles in 3 sweeps with slip rate 0.77 and keeps its state (memory).\nHow it assembles (32 copies, 2 worlds): 1.09 bonds per particle, largest molecule 5.5 particles, 10.5 left free.\n\nIts matter vs an ESN of the same size (readout only, both with tuned input scale); controls: no bonds, random bonds:\n  memcap   0.114 vs ESN 1.000\u00b10.000  [below]  no bonds 0.116  random bonds 0.117\n  xor      0.585 vs ESN 0.665\u00b10.237  [match]  no bonds 0.600  random bonds 0.562\n  narma10  0.360 vs ESN 0.842\u00b10.001  [below]  no bonds 0.360  random bonds 0.337\n  latch    0.895 vs ESN 0.677\u00b10.009  [beat]  no bonds 0.875  random bonds 0.886\n  mnist14  0.601 vs ESN 0.839\u00b10.006  [below]  no bonds 0.552  random bonds 0.601\n\nGood at: xor, latch.\nIts assembly does not matter: the particles do as well unbonded, so the useful part is the single particle's own stick-slip memory.\nUse (a guess): combining recent events nonlinearly (temporal feature detection); holding a value until it is overwritten, with nothing trained but the readout: a physical memory element for a recurrent or reservoir system."
